  "profile": {
    "video_id": "iron-snail:FbAkrx4mzg0",
    "purpose": "Explains what makes Limmer's Bavarian-style hiking boots special: their construction, fit and history, and who owns the company now. It is framed around the creator's quest to get a pair of custom Limmers for Rob, an 80-year-old trail maintainer who had wanted a pair for about 70 years.",
    "video_type": "explainer",
    "thesis": "Limmer boots are a stiff, snug, heavily built boot made for trail builders, rangers and people carrying heavy loads. The brand's history and its current owners keep it a small, tradition-driven workshop rather than a mass-market boot company.",
    "key_takeaways": [
      "Limmer upper construction: one single piece of leather with a six-stitch seam on the inside rather than a seam at the back.",
      "Limmer has two programs. The stock boots are owned by Chris, made in Bavaria by Meindl, faster to get and cheaper. The custom boots are made by Adam and crew in Intervale, New Hampshire, cost more and take longer.",
      "Stock models discussed: the Lightweight and the Standard, which is built for New Hampshire Fish and Game search and rescue.",
      "Construction of the Standard boot: a 2.2 mm leather lining, a 3.6 mm leather heel counter and a 3.2 mm leather outer.",
      "Limmer fitting practice: boots are sized snug, foam locks the heel, lacing does not run to the toe, and staff tie the boots very tight.",
      "Brand history: Peter Limmer Sr., a Bavarian master bootmaker, emigrated to Jamaica Plain, patented a US ski boot in 1939, moved to the White Mountains and partnered with his friend Lukas Meindl Sr.",
      "Ownership: the Limmer family declined buyout offers and eventually sold to Adam, a former banker who apprenticed under Pete Limmer, and to Chris, a former Philmont ranger and lifelong fan.",
      "Custom process: a foot tracing, with an archive that includes the tracings of famous customers.",
      "Rob's experience: he found the boots stiff at first, but on a wet descent he found them grippy and stable.",
      "Sponsor: Vinted."
    ],
    "outline": [
      {
        "title": "The legacy of Limmer boots",
        "summary": "Introduces Limmer as the boot all hiking boots are compared to, citing Randy Merrell, and teases Rob's story. Begins with the single-piece leather upper and its unusual six-stitch side seam.",
        "first_segment": 0,
        "last_segment": 1
      },
      {
        "title": "Obsession and precision",
        "summary": "Explains the inside seam, Limmer's insistence on teaching customers proper fit and lacing, and the Lightweight vs Standard models. Lists famous Limmer wearers.",
        "first_segment": 2,
        "last_segment": 4
      },
      {
        "title": "Sponsorship and Vinted",
        "summary": "Sponsor read for Vinted, with the creator listing pieces from his studio stockpile for sale.",
        "first_segment": 4,
        "last_segment": 6
      },
      {
        "title": "Meeting Rob and his dream",
        "summary": "How the creator met Rob, who had wanted Limmers since the 1950s, and the plan to get him a pair. Includes the visits to the workshop and to Chris, Adam and Kaminski.",
        "first_segment": 6,
        "last_segment": 9
      },
      {
        "title": "The Bavarian roots",
        "summary": "History of Peter Limmer Sr., his move from Bavaria to Jamaica Plain and then the White Mountains, the 1939 ski boot patent, and the Bavarian-influenced ski culture. Covers the partnership with Lukas Meindl Sr.",
        "first_segment": 9,
        "last_segment": 13
      },
      {
        "title": "Rob's life and health scare",
        "summary": "Rob's background in trail maintenance and as a water ski instructor, his introduction to the Limmer team, and his stroke and recovery.",
        "first_segment": 13,
        "last_segment": 17
      },
      {
        "title": "The custom boot process",
        "summary": "How Adam went from banker to Limmer owner, and the foot tracing process and archive. Gives the layer-by-layer leather specification of the Standard boot.",
        "first_segment": 17,
        "last_segment": 21
      },
      {
        "title": "Designing for durability",
        "summary": "Why Limmers are heavy and stiff for load-carrying stability. Covers Chris's background and the snug, heel-locking fit with its handshake-like hold and partial lacing.",
        "first_segment": 21,
        "last_segment": 26
      },
      {
        "title": "Breaking in the boots",
        "summary": "How tight Limmer staff lace boots, Rob's worried first reaction to the stiff Lightweights, and his grumpy break-in emails.",
        "first_segment": 26,
        "last_segment": 30
      },
      {
        "title": "The mountain test",
        "summary": "The group hike on Rob's trail. Rob describes how much grip and stability the boots gave him on a wet descent as he passed younger hikers.",
        "first_segment": 30,
        "last_segment": 33
      },
      {
        "title": "The philosophy of the brand",
        "summary": "Limmer's aim that boots be worn for a long time, and Adam's offer of boots to young trail-building volunteers. Contrasts Limmer with Merrell and covers who the family chose to sell to.",
        "first_segment": 34,
        "last_segment": 37
      },
      {
        "title": "Preserving the tradition",
        "summary": "Adam pulls old family tracings from the archive for visitors, and reflects on the past. Rob agrees to will his boots back to the shop for a young trail builder.",
        "first_segment": 38,
        "last_segment": 42
      }
    ],
